\section{Research attempt: A snippet interface must include its free dependencies}
In a finite expression language, a snippet is built from literals, named references, and concatenation. Its free-dependency set is computed recursively: a literal has none; a reference to $a$ has $\{a\}$; concatenation has the union of its children's sets. If two environments agree on every name in that set, the snippet has the same value in both. Proof is structural induction: literal values agree; a referenced name agrees by hypothesis; concatenation of equal component values agrees. This establishes an exact residual-context interface for the declared language.

The interface is needed even for a snippet that looks self-contained. The reference $a$ can render as 2 in one environment and 3 in another. Copying only the reference cannot determine which value was intended. If environment values themselves are macro expressions rather than already evaluated strings, the dependency calculation must recurse through those definitions; a one-level name list can miss $a\to b\to c$.

Name dependencies do not exhaust every possible context. Extend the language with a token that prints the ambient theorem counter. Its output differs between counter states 4 and 5 even when all named strings agree. The prior interface theorem then fails because it omitted a newly observable input. The repair is to include that counter in the environment interface and prove the additional syntax case. This gives a concrete boundary to the strategy: an interface certificate is sound only when its dependency analysis covers every observable form in the accepted snippet grammar.
